#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/id #uuid "e4893301-34d3-5f63-9d1c-715588d98d40" :question/math-academy-id "e-9531" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "The notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ is used to remind us that we're dealing with a line integral over a *closed* curve.\n\nWe will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t)+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179528 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "a448227d-9bf3-54dd-8639-cbdc56a5a403" :question/math-academy-id "e-9532" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the curve $y^3 = x-5$ traversed from the point $A(5,0)$ to the point $B(6,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^3 = x-5\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\nx = y^3 + 5.\n$$\n\nSo, we can parametrize the curve using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{3}+5)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^3+5, \\qquad\ny = t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+({t}^{3}+5)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}+{t}^{3}+5 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{3}+5\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{4}+5t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+5-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/id #uuid "9152629f-47a8-5a71-9bd9-3f5205788f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9533" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) =(1-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(0, 2)$ to the point $B(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$\\mathbf{a} = 2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{i}.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =2j+t[i-2j] \\\\  & =t\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t = 1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\n\ty = 2-2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(1-y)^{2}\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =(1-(2-2t))^{2}\\,i+t\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-1)^{2}\\,i+t\\,j \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-2t)j \\\\  & =i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-2j) \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\cdot (1)+(t)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-6t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}-6t+1\\,dt \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}-3+1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95e28c9e-2228-50d4-b676-f6484c1b9e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}e^{3 - 2t} + \\ln (2 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177647} {:answer/id #uuid "10691e6c-af5a-594a-b543-d69d9c8da3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}e^{2 + 3t} + \\ln (3 - 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177648} {:answer/id #uuid "2050ccfb-94cc-5c91-b0d9-4c7a9da71943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}e^{-2t} + \\ln (3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177649} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc7f10b-b993-57a8-9231-98c8d45d434a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}3e^{2 + 3t} - 2\\ln (3 - 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177650} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff8c510-3cf3-51a4-8eff-d968f9f33374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1} - 2e^{3 - 2t} + 3\\ln (2 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ff8c510-3cf3-51a4-8eff-d968f9f33374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1} - 2e^{3 - 2t} + 3\\ln (2 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177651} :answer-field/id #uuid "a261db06-f27f-544f-817f-62483c77b794" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88b31475-15e6-5485-a3fc-dfd5113d0f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-124701" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(3,2)$ to the point $B(1,5)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(3-2t)\\,i+(2+3t)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3\\,i+2\\,j$ and $b=i+5\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(3\\,i+2\\,j)+t[(i+5\\,j)-(3\\,i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(3\\,i+2\\,j)+t(-2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =(3-2t)\\,i+(2+3t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3-2t,\\,y=2+3t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{3-2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+3t)j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+3\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{3-2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{3-2t}\\cdot (-2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{3-2t}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{3-2t}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6ea158e-ca2a-5e5b-8ecd-b2fa2a21a9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177653} {:answer/id #uuid "e8439413-3db2-5587-a745-3d8cdeed25d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177654} {:answer/id #uuid "eae912eb-e824-5f79-b270-5672633c8fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177655} {:answer/id #uuid "0befd065-e9be-574b-abba-b2bce297a583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177656} {:answer/id #uuid "53a8508e-7bbf-53fe-9d00-f271624998ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eae912eb-e824-5f79-b270-5672633c8fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177655} :answer-field/id #uuid "578f5370-21a2-5f59-b58d-cbb06fd9058c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0f71dad-242f-524e-855f-4cf34a64efd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124702" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i-j$ and $b=-3\\,i+j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(i-j)+t[-3\\,i+j-(i-j)] \\\\  & =(i-j)+t(-4\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(1-4t)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=1-4t,\\,y=2t-1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y\\,j \\\\  & =(1-4t+(2t-1))\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =-2t\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-4t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)j \\\\  & =-4\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-2t\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,j)\\cdot (-4\\,i+2j) \\\\  & =-2t\\cdot (-4)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8t+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8t+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,dt \\\\  & ={4{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =(4+0)-(0+0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fa2e1ba-2f69-5ed9-b1da-a845dff72e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337226} {:answer/id #uuid "ee78efa8-dce3-52b0-ab74-e09874f04f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337227} {:answer/id #uuid "786d7521-de9b-56ca-8f66-5b3e2469ac83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{176}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337228} {:answer/id #uuid "acfe085e-cfe6-53e2-9c21-d550eca63585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337229} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0dbb8f-44cf-5bdb-bb5a-c1da7bd67ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acfe085e-cfe6-53e2-9c21-d550eca63585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337229} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b931f22-8f52-51fa-890c-f3a8b991fc7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cb93a673-1648-507b-9701-d342ceacf5ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-124706" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve ${y}^{3}=x+3$ traversed from the point $A(-3,0)$ to the point $B(5,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=x+3\\,⟹\\,x={y}^{3}-3.\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{3}-3)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-3)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{3}-3))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}-3)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-3)\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{5}-9{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{5}-8{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{t}^{5}-8{t}^{2})\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{2}-\\frac{8{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{6}}{2}-\\frac{8(2)^{3}}{3})-0 \\\\  & =32-\\frac{64}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "634d5245-4fc4-547b-ad06-1b8f84de01c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337233} {:answer/id #uuid "9829acf0-3155-5818-b47e-5c3a09f2487a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337234} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c23fb5-e632-58a0-9012-319a7cd0c67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337235} {:answer/id #uuid "6156d68f-3feb-5330-965e-7d0437d9aa14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337236} {:answer/id #uuid "28152fcb-c9de-57a7-b654-9ee664d95674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1c23fb5-e632-58a0-9012-319a7cd0c67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337235} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a31baf7-c788-5adb-8618-75311d27fcd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1433a248-76d2-5a14-b81b-062f9386a211" :question/math-academy-id "q-125027" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=3{x}^{2}+1$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =t\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+1)j \\\\  & =i+6t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+6t\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 1+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =t+18{t}^{3}+6t \\\\  & =7t+18{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7t+18{t}^{3})\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{7(1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9(1)^{4}}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "868724c5-e34e-5f63-97f9-e5b667d01d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177683} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dd77e4-7247-5b7b-810e-79429371f3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177684} {:answer/id #uuid "303520fd-35f6-50fc-89df-965454ca9e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177685} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa3fad4-e00a-5302-b124-b4827f0026a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177686} {:answer/id #uuid "61d1a130-0e5c-5439-bf25-21822e3e1437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "868724c5-e34e-5f63-97f9-e5b667d01d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177683} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7547657-ebfa-5914-a3aa-74dcfd5d7b8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9565c624-6479-5e2a-bbcb-90464f68b7c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-125039" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{sin}^{2}⁡t)j \\\\  & =i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\text{ })\\cdot (i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2{sin}^{4}⁡t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6c158ee-dd34-5183-80ea-1f31735174c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3(2+3t)^{7}+4\\cdot {5}^{5+4t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337240} {:answer/id #uuid "0b79a014-acf2-5f8a-ad71-974a5e98c40f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+3t)^{7}+{5}^{5+4t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337241} {:answer/id #uuid "486ed8fb-0a87-52fe-97f6-505103f1752b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}6(3t)^{7}+{5}^{4t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337242} {:answer/id #uuid "a93ba21f-a81e-5b99-afa6-11e330ab5783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}6(2+3t)^{7}+{5}^{5+4t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337243} {:answer/id #uuid "509e6ab5-20ac-54f3-bf91-2b37b89e7d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}4(5+4t)^{7}+3\\cdot {5}^{2+3t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a93ba21f-a81e-5b99-afa6-11e330ab5783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}6(2+3t)^{7}+{5}^{5+4t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337243} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbc2fd45-07e9-5bd3-90b2-d89713a73939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d5fc532-7b7f-5d7a-b4ea-98b60f4db480" :question/math-academy-id "q-125048" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=2{x}^{7}\\,i+{5}^{x+y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(2,3)$ to the point $B(5,4)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(2+3t)\\,i+(3+t)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=2\\,i+3\\,j$ and $b=5\\,i+4\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i+3\\,j)+t[(5\\,i+4\\,j)-(2\\,i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i+3\\,j)+t(3\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(2+3t)\\,i+(3+t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2+3t,\\,y=3+t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2{x}^{7}\\,i+{5}^{x+y}\\,j \\\\  & =2(2+3t)^{7}\\,i+{5}^{2+3t+3+t}\\,j \\\\  & =2(2+3t)^{7}\\,i+{5}^{5+4t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2(2+3t)^{7}\\,i+{5}^{5+4t}\\,j)\\cdot (3\\,i+j) \\\\  & =2(2+3t)^{7}\\cdot 3+{5}^{5+4t}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6(2+3t)^{7}+{5}^{5+4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6(2+3t)^{7}+{5}^{5+4t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eddf7318-bcdc-544c-9366-2fa0ea099e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337247} {:answer/id #uuid "8a77048d-c0f3-5fc7-8bb8-950b61209321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337248} {:answer/id #uuid "b192fff1-59d4-5461-9f75-32907119420a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337249} {:answer/id #uuid "cfcca7d2-c3ab-5469-b077-100117be7e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337250} {:answer/id #uuid "d19c2323-4547-5e32-a4ea-2808da118247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfcca7d2-c3ab-5469-b077-100117be7e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337250} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ee28a0b-51f8-5b4a-9f9e-ec0e5f62633a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4dada09-aabc-5091-a725-8e79233cd8fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-125049" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=y\\,i+x\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=x-2$ traversed from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(2,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=x-2\\,⟹\\,x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2.\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+j) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2)\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2t]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9714d082-0de3-500b-a046-1ae4838f3619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337254} {:answer/id #uuid "5396ae12-fb7a-579d-92df-d06788d3109b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337255} {:answer/id #uuid "87e0d9b5-1bd2-5b02-b3cc-ba2f225cbbc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337256} {:answer/id #uuid "cab3d3b9-66a9-5bd0-9c70-02bc15f3b3bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337257} {:answer/id #uuid "79684ba5-533a-53e4-9785-0c04082569c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5396ae12-fb7a-579d-92df-d06788d3109b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337255} :answer-field/id #uuid "75903ef4-aa02-5ad6-b03a-671cfaf4c011" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53661f2e-8ed4-5537-92f1-dab42a761ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-125052" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{x+y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=j$ and $b=3\\,i.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(j)+t(3\\,i-j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(1-t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3t\\,y=1-t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{x+y}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{x+y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3t+1-t}\\,i+\\frac{2-2t}{3t+1-t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\,i+\\frac{2-2t}{2t+1}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\,i+\\frac{2-2t}{2t+1}\\,j)\\cdot (3\\,i-j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\cdot 3+\\frac{2-2t}{2t+1}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3-2+2t}{2t+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1\\,dt \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06826d6a-efa4-5b39-9d10-136176abd710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{2}-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337261} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0f0f33-1bfc-5283-8de9-47e8e56f96dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337262} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb500e3-4eea-59d4-8112-3de1186c346e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{2}+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337263} {:answer/id #uuid "3ac3f6d7-735b-5da0-890d-de97ad726408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337264} {:answer/id #uuid "9609fd9f-45ee-57f4-92e8-6ae8c1fc8cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9609fd9f-45ee-57f4-92e8-6ae8c1fc8cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337265} :answer-field/id #uuid "982853ad-bf1d-5d0a-b707-d63d704e2c0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c2abf40-1e9c-5ba7-adbb-95d58c38ba59" :question/math-academy-id "q-125053" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=2\\,i$ and $b=4\\,i+2\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i)+t[(4\\,i+2\\,j)-(2\\,i)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i)+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+2)\\,i+2t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2t+2\\,y=2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\,i+{e}^{2t+2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{2t}\\,i+{e}^{2t+2}\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\cdot 2+{e}^{2t+2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(1+{e}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{2t}(1+{e}^{2})\\,dt \\\\  & =(1+{e}^{2}){e}^{2t}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+{e}^{2})({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{4}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4c9b433-6649-5e3f-9efe-731be1d0fe94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337268} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac52df3-5347-5d5f-bd29-da69da812345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337269} {:answer/id #uuid "ea37045e-ebcb-5535-8edc-754c453c21bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337270} {:answer/id #uuid "fe720504-8c22-575f-be82-9f6d92fdf877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337271} {:answer/id #uuid "68063a49-18e3-52ae-a526-e1090f08375d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68063a49-18e3-52ae-a526-e1090f08375d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337272} :answer-field/id #uuid "a943557a-c806-5351-947d-3780140e7556" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "18de9645-42ff-5489-8ee4-05311c82af19" :question/math-academy-id "q-125066" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\,i+x\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve ${y}^{2}=\\frac{x-1}{3}$ traversed from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=\\frac{x-1}{3}\\,⟹\\,x=3{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2}+1,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2{y}^{2}\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =6t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j)\\cdot (6t\\,i+j) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot 6t+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={[3{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3+1+1-0 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f012ecd5-a690-5960-b2c5-c541dc251517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337275} {:answer/id #uuid "7c924b8b-11b3-54a7-b55c-9d3a48d43d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337276} {:answer/id #uuid "2018c8c5-3dc3-56b1-8f09-671ecdc52483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337277} {:answer/id #uuid "1e3b81dd-555f-58a9-8cd0-0beb58d7263d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337278} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb8e708-fffa-5eff-8101-53e02c851cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bb8e708-fffa-5eff-8101-53e02c851cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337279} :answer-field/id #uuid "89b8ae8d-0e8c-53ad-b893-6a419e85cf23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60a03e43-d690-535e-81af-00842b63c1aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-125170" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058907/assets/00ff3216e6876673a388eb3eaa0e7d02e268f5e679593ebd9fe01e2840099e1b.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+xy\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin. This curve can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+xy\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b58a2a8d-0cf0-50a4-ac35-6fb047b213b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(6{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337282} {:answer/id #uuid "e76b78ed-042f-520e-95b2-88f780dbdac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(3{cos}^{2}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337283} {:answer/id #uuid "e0305b59-c6fb-55df-bb89-5bcfef1b9f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337284} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2d422e-0d9b-512b-8528-012ccfa2e4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337285} {:answer/id #uuid "437119a0-1cd0-5ed1-9576-1eac64655a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(6{cos}^{2}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b58a2a8d-0cf0-50a4-ac35-6fb047b213b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(6{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337282} :answer-field/id #uuid "e07d2f66-ad11-5972-bd00-9b461d52a570" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "650eb0a9-0597-5e63-9ed1-143928ee022a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125171" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058907/assets/f7bacafcbe893c0d21ea45a916190a49aa3a3532834cff4704f707c100bf8e6a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=3y\\,i+2x\\,j$ and $C$ is the the arc of the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3$ respectively, traversed anticlockwise from $A(0,3)$ to $B(-1,0)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the parametrization of an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3$ respectively. It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =3y\\,i+2x\\,j \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-9{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =6{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(6{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67e04a99-9ba3-5a87-9960-6c84034ffb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337289} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5cc75f-60c1-56ab-8b49-fd0000583386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337290} {:answer/id #uuid "197282f6-6e2a-5d7d-9a5d-7d78ec79eddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337291} {:answer/id #uuid "047ea37a-fb73-58e6-a81b-3ab493b3292e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337292} {:answer/id #uuid "e2146c88-cbbd-5789-9ee1-9a23d478517c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047ea37a-fb73-58e6-a81b-3ab493b3292e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337292} :answer-field/id #uuid "f679b7f0-ecd9-5b84-bbd5-9df73587621c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b794b22-45f0-563f-b97c-2452d92d75df" :question/math-academy-id "q-125238" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i-{e}^{y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin. This curve can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{x}\\,i-{e}^{y}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi /2}-({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}) \\\\  & =1-e-(e-1) \\\\  & =2-2e\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "873039ce-760f-5e07-bf69-f067083634e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337296} {:answer/id #uuid "5f09d875-9ae6-5bab-af23-f750c0f60d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337297} {:answer/id #uuid "215d2d10-536d-50ef-afc2-5126679076d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337298} {:answer/id #uuid "47c24a06-7f1c-5dc3-bb44-5c2e291e0bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337299} {:answer/id #uuid "ad82228f-cb5c-58b8-855e-5462618692c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad82228f-cb5c-58b8-855e-5462618692c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337300} :answer-field/id #uuid "eacd4914-4207-55e6-80e2-b9ed89b1766f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66073e06-bf27-5b3e-854c-8243f5b08a5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39427" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058907/assets/c5e026312ab8a2ccfc61a37246c954b56550316661f158743cc2c46411a97f53.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(y-x)\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=2$  traversed counterclockwise from the point $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Notice that we can write its equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our curve is a part-ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$ respectively. This curve can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-x)\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\sqrt{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\sqrt{2}t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7249a807-6ff2-54c4-a16a-b61f250cc890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337303} {:answer/id #uuid "aee03c5a-ff26-5f14-a727-ec8a36825f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337304} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd98742-d423-5cf9-9d8b-259c8fdaed94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337305} {:answer/id #uuid "0951eace-a749-5162-8f53-4a81be033b09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337306} {:answer/id #uuid "acebada4-b4b6-5491-8051-df26d956acee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acebada4-b4b6-5491-8051-df26d956acee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337307} :answer-field/id #uuid "6053eec2-e5a9-5f33-8b7e-ef95ac7eed27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d10387d-6cca-597b-add1-a27be5676ce8" :question/math-academy-id "q-77034" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058907/assets/00547d8271d0c277de2bd1992d759e0157a80c9caf05cfb1020550853ae7b8b3.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(1-x)\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$  traversed from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Notice that we can write its equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our curve is a part-ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $3$ respectively. This curve can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+(1-x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-6({sin}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-6)\\,dt \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6t]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[1+0-6\\pi ]-[-1+3-3\\pi ] \\\\  & =-1-3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
